**LRU: data structures.** A `HashMap<K, Node>` for O(1) lookup, and a **doubly linked list** ordered by recency (head = most recent, tail = least recent). Use dummy head and tail nodes to avoid null checks.

```java
class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> map = new HashMap<>();
    private final Node<K, V> head = new Node<>(null, null), tail = new Node<>(null, null);
    LRUCache(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; head.next = tail; tail.prev = head; }
    V get(K key) {
        Node<K, V> n = map.get(key);
        if (n == null) return null;
        unlink(n); addFront(n); return n.val;
    }
    void put(K key, V val) {
        Node<K, V> n = map.get(key);
        if (n != null) { n.val = val; unlink(n); addFront(n); return; }
        if (map.size() == capacity) { Node<K, V> lru = tail.prev; unlink(lru); map.remove(lru.key); }
        n = new Node<>(key, val); map.put(key, n); addFront(n);
    }
}
```
`unlink` and `addFront` are four-pointer updates. The node stores its key so eviction can remove the map entry. Both operations are O(1), space O(capacity). Validate capacity > 0, and discuss `LinkedHashMap` with `accessOrder` as the one-line alternative, but implement manually when asked.

**OOP design:** hide the list behind a small `EvictionPolicy` interface (`onAccess`, `onInsert`, `evict`), so `Cache` handles storage and the policy decides what to remove. This makes LFU a new policy rather than a rewrite. Thread safety: lock around get and put, since `get` also mutates order. A `ConcurrentHashMap` alone is not enough.

**LFU extension.** Keep `Map<K, Node>` plus `Map<Integer, DoublyLinkedList>` keyed by frequency and a `minFreq` variable. On access: remove the node from its list for freq f, put it at the front of the list for f+1, and if list f is now empty and `minFreq == f`, increment `minFreq`. On insert at capacity: evict the **tail of the `minFreq` list** (least frequently used, and among ties the least recently used), insert the new node with frequency 1 and set `minFreq = 1`. All operations are O(1).

**Edge cases:** capacity 0, updating an existing key (counts as an access), null keys, equal frequencies, and a key evicted then re-inserted starting at frequency 1.
